    },

    entry: {
      search: "Book Search",
      hexguide_patterns: "HexGuide Patterns",
      guide: {
        stack: "The Stack, the Grid, and the Casting Environment",
        iotas: "Iotas",
        actions: "Patterns, Actions, and Spells",
        spellcasting: "Practice Spellcasting",
        escape: "Introspection, Consideration, and Retrospection",
        foreach: "Thoth's Gambit Explained",
        meta: "Meta-Execution and the Mechanics of Casting",
        logics: "Logic Branching",
        demo: "Spellcasting Demo",
      },
      casting: {
        hexguide: "About The HexBook",
      },
    },

    page: {
      search: {
        "0": "Below is a text search box for the contents and entries of The HexBook. Simply type in a name, keyword, or natural language description to find relevant entries. Click any result to jump directly to that page.",
      },
      guide: {
        iotas: {
          "0": "Hex Casting is a craft that uses $(media)media/$ to manipulate Iotas, and through them the world itself, rewriting reality. Iotas are the \"names\" of all things; we use patterns to obtain Iotas, change their types, compute with them, or execute them — all to draw the information (Iotas) we want from Nature. Then we feed that information to patterns in the right form to achieve the effect we desire. Many patterns combined can create spells as powerful as works of art.",
          "1": "There are many kinds of Iotas: vectors iota:hexguide:vec0.json, numbers iota:double0.json, entities, patterns, booleans iota:bool0.json, and lists iota:list0.json, among others. Different types carry different meanings: a vector Iota usually represents a position, direction, or velocity; a number Iota is just a number. Most special of all is the Pattern Iota — it stands for a pattern that might itself be cast — and a list of patterns usually means a whole spell made of meaningful patterns. For example iota:list0.json",
          "2": "Besides those mentioned above, Garbage Iotas like iota:garbage.json and iota:null.json are also common.",
        },
        actions: {
          "0": "Hex casters manipulate Iotas, and through them the objective world. The tools used to manipulate, multiply, consume, and transform Iotas — and thereby rewrite reality — are what this text calls $(thing)Actions$(). In general, most $(thing)Actions$() correspond to a meaningful $(thing)pattern$(); by drawing those patterns on the grid, we can execute the corresponding action. If an action cannot actually be executed, you will run into a $(thing)mishap$(). This makes it clear: patterns do not always correspond to actions, so there are patterns with no meaning — draw them and you will suffer a mishap.",
          "1": "Likewise, not every action that can be performed has a corresponding pattern, but I will not go into that here. Next come Spells. In the narrow sense, a $(thing)Spell$() refers to the series of patterns in the Spells section of this book that affect the world — for example $(l:hexcasting:patterns/spells/basic#hexcasting:explode)$(action)Explosion/$. Most spells merely consume Iotas, performing no complex manipulation of them, while costing more or less media. In a broader sense, hex casters also call any carefully crafted list of patterns a \"spell\" or a \"hexcast\". Understanding and distinguishing these concepts is important for learning and mastering Hex Casting.",
          "2": "The essence of casting should then be obvious: use patterns like $(l:hexcasting:patterns/basics#hexcasting:get_caster)$(action)Mind's Reflection/$ and $(l:hexcasting:patterns/basics#hexcasting:raycast)$(action)Archer's Distillation/$ to draw Iotas from the world, use $(l:hexcasting:patterns/stackmanip)stack manipulation$() patterns such as Jester's Gambit and Gemini Decomposition to arrange the order, quantity, and types of Iotas on the stack to suit the spell, then execute the spell pattern. That is the fundamental method of casting.",
        },
        stack: {
          "0": "The place where hex casters manipulate Iotas is the $(l:hexcasting:casting/stack)stack$(). A stack is \"last in, first out\" — like a pile of plates, stacked one on top of another; you take from the top. The so-called spellcasting grid — simply a canvas — is where patterns are drawn. Drawing the right pattern on the $(l:hexcasting:guide/stack#hexguide:spellcasting)grid$() executes the corresponding action. Right-click with a staff to open the grid; sneak-right-click to open it while clearing both the canvas and the stack. The stack is shown in the top-left corner of the grid.",
          "1": "While casting with a staff you can see the stack directly, so you can draw patterns flexibly to perform the actions you want and release fitting spells.$(br2)But sometimes that is not so convenient, so hex casters prepare a $(action)pattern list$() in advance and write it into a $(l:hexcasting:items/hexcasting)casting item$() or focus, to cast quickly. So what is the difference between casting from an item and casting from a staff? The biggest difference is the $(thing)casting environment$() — it determines the caster, the source of media, the casting hand, and other vital information.",
          "2": "For example, a staff's casting environment is the staff environment, which uses media from your inventory; a $(l:hexcasting:items/ancient_cyphers)cypher's$() environment can only use the media stored within itself.$(br2)In general, each cast creates a new environment carrying new information (the ancients called it a $(thing)Casting Image$()), so each use of a casting item does not inherit the previous stack, Raven's Thoughts, impetus marks, and so on. A staff, however, has a special property: every time you open the grid, it inherits the previous stack and Raven's Thoughts.",
          "3": "Strangely enough, by linking this $(thing)HexBook$() to my mind, it can act much like a staff. Click the Cast button and patterns drawn on the grid will take effect just like a normal staff; click it again to $(l:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas)escape$() all patterns drawn afterwards, pushing them onto the stack. The Clr button clears the canvas.",
        },
        escape: {
          "0": "$(action)Consideration, Introspection, and Retrospection/$ are arguably the three most special patterns of all. Most actions manipulate the stack and the world; these three instead change the \"state\" of the casting.$(br2)In general, a pattern executes as soon as it is drawn. But if you draw Consideration or Introspection beforehand, subsequently drawn patterns turn yellow, marking them as \"escaped\". $(l:hexcasting:patterns/patterns_as_iotas)See here for a detailed description of patterns/$.",
          "1": "$(thing)Escaping$() means making an Iota lose its original meaning — instead of executing its corresponding action, it is pushed as a mere Iota into a list or onto the stack.$(br2)In Hex Casting, every Iota has meaning; you might say every Iota corresponds to an \"action\", except that for most Iotas that action is \"throw a mishap\". So executing them directly with $(l:hexcasting:patterns/meta)$(action)Hermes' Gambit/$ or Thoth's Gambit throws a mishap.",
          "2": "Thus the secret of Hermes is revealed:$(br2)When Hermes receives a non-list Iota — say, a Pattern Iota — it executes the corresponding \"action\" directly; when it receives a list, it executes the action of each Iota in turn, as if drawing the patterns one by one on the grid.$(br2)Note that a list Iota's own action is merely to throw a mishap, so a list like iota:list1.json also throws a mishap under Hermes' Gambit.",
          "3": "Escaping has another use: embedding non-pattern Iotas inside patterns. The simplest way is Consideration, written as a list like iota:list2.json. However, Consideration takes priority over Introspection, so it does not work well inside layers of Introspection and Retrospection.$(br2)A more general method of escaping looks like$(br)iota:list3.json$(br)First pack the Iotas into a list with Introspection and Retrospection, then use $(l:hexcasting:patterns/lists#hexcasting:splat)$(action)Flock's Disintegration/$ to unpack the list, pushing its elements onto the stack.",
        },
        foreach: {
          "0": "$(l:hexcasting:patterns/meta#hexcasting:for_each)$(action)Thoth's Gambit/$ looks complex but is actually very simple: it takes two lists — the first is the $(hex)pattern list$(), the second may be called the target list.$(br2)Thoth's Gambit executes the actions in the first list against every element of the second list, with one small twist. For easy understanding: Thoth's Gambit is like working in several stacks identical to the current one,",
          "1": "pushing one element of the first list into each of them, then executing the actions given by the pattern list in every new stack, and finally gathering the results — all elements of all those stacks — into a single list returned to the original stack.$(br2)For example: suppose the stack holds iota:210983.json; push $(l:hexcasting:patterns/math#hexcasting:add)$(action)Additive Distillation iota:5f5abf.json/$ and then iota:8cac19.json, then use Thoth's Gambit. For every Iota, it creates a new stack identical to the original, pushes the Iota into it, giving$(br)iota:74fc83.json$(br)iota:a5f05d.json$(br)iota:d37a8c.json$(br)then executes iota:5f5abf.json in each of them,",
          "2": "collecting the results separately, the overall result packed together is:$(br)iota:aa94c6.json$(br2)Thoth's Gambit is mainly used for repeated operations — for example, breaking blocks over a large area, or applying negative effects to all entities in a range. For instance, this spell$(br)iota:1fcc1e.json$(br)first uses $(l:hexcasting:patterns/entities#hexcasting:zone_entity/player)$(action)Zone Distillation: Player/$ to get all entities in range, and $(l:hexcasting:patterns/stackmanip#hexcasting:swap)$(action)Jester's Gambit/$ to arrange them. Then Thoth's Gambit accelerates each of them once. You can try Thoth's Gambit on the $(l:hexcasting:guide/stack#hexguide:spellcasting)grid/$ to the right.",
        },
        meta: {
          "0": "Meta-execution hides the deepest mysteries of Hex Casting; to understand Hex Casting, you must understand meta-execution. From the notes, we can see that $(l:hexcasting:patterns/meta)$(action)Hermes' Gambit/$ pops a pattern or pattern list from the top of the stack and runs it.$(br2)That is easy to grasp. Yet at the end of these notes, $(l:hexcasting:patterns/meta#hexcasting:halt)$(action)Charon's Gambit/$ is also mentioned — it can stop a cast, but unlike simply lowering the staff or having a mishap interrupt the cast, it can stop those \"gambits\".",
          "1": "Stop the \"gambits\"? What does that mean? What is the difference between a pattern executed by meta-execution and one drawn one by one with a staff?$(br2)To explain, we need a basic understanding of the $(hex)mechanics of casting$(). For a single cast, the place that stores the stack, Raven's Thoughts, and other basic data is called the $(hex)Casting Image$(); what stores all the operations the caster is about to perform is called the $(thing)Continuation$().$(br2)When casting by drawing directly with a staff, the caster never told Nature in advance what operations they would perform, so there is no need to consider continuations here.",
          "2": "When we use meta-execution like Hermes' Gambit to run a pattern list, a marker is inserted into the current continuation, and then every operation in the pattern list is inserted after that marker in order. The operations inserted afterwards — those about to be performed — are collectively called the $(thing)Execution Frame$(). The marker and the execution frame together form a $(thing)Casting Boundary$().$(br2)And so the mystery of meta-execution is revealed: Hermes' Gambit creates a casting boundary and pushes an execution frame; if Charon's Gambit is executed inside the frame, it climbs up the continuation, finds the marker Hermes inserted, ends the cast within the boundary,",
          "3": "leaps out of the current meta-execution frame, and continues with any later operations.$(br2)Take iota:d04cdc.json, for example:$(br)Mind's Reflection and Reveal will be executed, while Compass' Purification and Reveal after Charon's Gambit will not.$(br2)Charon works with Thoth's Gambit much the same way: when Charon is met inside Thoth's Gambit, Thoth's traversal stops and execution returns to the level above Thoth.",
        },
        logics: {
          "0": "Different situations call for different spells — that is obvious. But relying on the caster to switch spells every time is hardly elegant, nor is it convenient. So we can use methods to implement $(thing)\"if...then...\"$() logic. The most common method uses $(l:hexcating:logic#hexcasting:if)$(action)Augur's Exaltation/$: based on the first boolean provided, it keeps either the third or the second element; combined with $(action)Hermes' Gambit/$, this yields an \"if...then...\" branch.",
          "1": "For example, this spell$(br)iota:5592af.json$(br)first uses Archer's Distillation to get the target coordinates, then $(l:hexcasting:stackmanip#hexcasting:duplicate)duplicates$() it and checks whether it is null with $(l:hexcasting:logic#hexcasting:equals)$(action)Equality Distillation/$; if so, it clears the stack with $(l:hexcasting:stackmamip#hexcasting:pseudo-novice)$(action)Novice's Gambit/$, otherwise it breaks the block. This spell has a more detailed $(l:hexcasting:guide/logics#play)demonstration/$ later on.$(br2)Beyond this method, you can also use $(l:hexcasting:lists#hexcasting:index)$(action)Selection Distillation/$ to pick different elements of a list by index for multi-way branching — not detailed here.",
        },
        demo: {
          "0": "This page demonstrates pattern drawing and the stack. Patterns appear on the grid one by one, and each drawn pattern is pushed onto the stack as a Pattern Iota shown in the top-left box. Use the buttons to pause or restart the demonstration."
        },
      },
      casting: {
        into: "\"I shall go and see, I shall go and know, I shall learn what I seek. Though the named shall return to the void, and the knowing shall be bound to the path, human toil yields nothing in the end — yet strive with all one's heart, and die halfway, facing the Great Work, with no regrets in life.\"",
        hexguide: "I use this notebook to record my understanding of Hex Casting, and in my studies it will always accompany me, from the very beginning to the very end.$(br2)I suspect the knowledge in this book may grow too vast and complex for any mind to grasp at once, so I am determined to write down my deeper understanding of the craft here. For those who come after...$(br2)$(o)But who will ever read my $(thing)notes$()? And who will read the $(l:hexcasting:guide)notes on the notes$()?$()",
      },
      hexguide_patterns: {
        copy: "$(l:hexcasting:guide/hexguide_patterns#hexguide:copy)$(action)Copy Iota/$ copies the top Iota of the stack to your clipboard and prints a clickable reference in chat, so you can share it or paste it into the book. It also saves the Iota as a JSON file under $(thing)hexguide/iotas/$ in the game directory, which can be displayed inline anywhere via a reference like $(br)$(code)iota:hash.json/$.",
        demo: "$(l:hexcasting:guide/hexguide_patterns#hexguide:demo)$(action)Save Demo/$ takes the list Iota on top of the stack (each Pattern Iota in the list becomes one step of the demo), generates a spellplay configuration file, and saves it under $(thing)hexguide/spellplays/$ in the game directory. Drop the generated file into a datapack at $(code)data/hexguide/spellplays/$ and it can be played on the Spellcasting Demo page of this book.",
      },
    },

